ADDRESS: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pier_(architecture)
==================================================
A pier, in  [LINK: (architecture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architecture] , is an upright support for a structure or  [LINK: (superstructure) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superstructure]  such as an  [LINK: (arch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch]  or  [LINK: (bridge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridge] . Sections of structural walls between openings (bays) can function as piers. External or free-standing walls may have piers at the ends or on corners.


# DESCRIPTION #

The simplest  [LINK: (cross section) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross_section_(geometry)]  of the pier is  [LINK: (square) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Square_(geometry)] , or  [LINK: (rectangular) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rectangle] , but other shapes are also common. In  [LINK: (medieval architecture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medieval_architecture] , massive  [LINK: (circular) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circle]  supports called drum piers,  [LINK: (cruciform) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cruciform]  (cross-shaped) piers, and  [LINK: (compound piers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compound_pier]  are common architectural elements.

[LINK: (Columns) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Column]  are a similar upright support, but stand on a round base; in many contexts columns may also be called piers. In buildings with a sequence of  [LINK: (bays) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay_(architecture)]  between piers, each opening (window or door) between two piers is considered a single bay.


# BRIDGE PIERS #

Single-span  [LINK: (bridges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridge]  have  [LINK: (abutments) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abutment]  at each end that support the weight of the bridge and serve as retaining walls to resist lateral movement of the earthen  [LINK: (fill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cut_and_fill]  of the bridge approach. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pier_(architecture)#cite_note-1]  Multi-span bridges require piers to support the ends of spans between these abutments. In cold climates, the upstream edge of a pier may include a  [LINK: (starkwater) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starling_(architecture)]  to prevent accumulation of broken ice during peak snowmelt flows. The starkwater has a sharpened upstream edge sometimes called a cutwater. The cutwater edge may be of concrete or masonry, but is often capped with a steel angle to resist abrasion and focus force at a single point to fracture floating pieces of ice striking the pier. In cold climates, the starling is typically sloped at an angle of about 45°  so current pushing against the ice tends to lift the downstream edge of the ice translating horizontal force of the current to vertical force against a thinner cross-section of ice until unsupported weight of ice fractures the piece of ice allowing it to pass on either side of the pier. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pier_(architecture)#cite_note-2]


# EXAMPLES #

In the  [LINK: (Arc de Triomphe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arc_de_Triomphe] , Paris (illustration, right) the central arch and side arches are raised on four massive planar piers[ [LINK: (clarification needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Please_clarify] ].

[LINK: (Donato Bramante) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donato_Bramante] 's original plan for  [LINK: (St Peter's Basilica) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Peter%27s_Basilica]  in Rome has richly articulated piers. Four piers support the weight of the  [LINK: (dome) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dome]  at the central crossing. These piers were found to be too small to support the weight and were changed later by  [LINK: (Michelangelo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michelangelo]  to account for the massive weight of the dome. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pier_(architecture)#cite_note-Fazio312-3]

The piers of the four  [LINK: (apses) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apse]  that project from each outer wall are also strong, to withstand the outward thrust of the half-domes upon them. Many  [LINK: (niches) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niche_(architecture)]  articulate the wall-spaces of the piers. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pier_(architecture)#cite_note-Fazio312-3]


